        title: "From Idea to Product-Market Fit',\n        content: 'Product-market fit (PMF) is the point at which a product satisfies a strong market demand — customers love it, use it repeatedly, and tell others about it. Before PMF, growth is forced and retention is poor — customers try and churn. After PMF, growth becomes easier — the product sells itself through word of mouth. Most early startup failures are pre-PMF companies that scaled too fast. Marc Andreessen (venture capitalist): \"The only thing that matters is getting to product-market fit.\" Signs you have PMF: high retention (customers keep using it), strong word of mouth (customers tell others without being asked), and a waiting list. Finding PMF requires iterating on the product based on intense customer feedback — often looking very different from the original idea.',\n        emoji: '🎯'",
